(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111300-eight-of-twenty-pilots-now-have-a-parity-harness")
  (:adr/topic "q9-parity-harness-coverage")
  (:adr/title "Eight of twenty pilots now have a parity harness, and the same row differs in every one")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609111200-coercion-coverage-is-kind-level-and-was-already-complete"
                "adr-2609110700-as-int-is-the-only-i64-divergence-in-the-wave-1-cohort"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

All twenty wave-1 pilots carry a whole-component build. Until this week four
carried a parity harness, so sixteen components had been built and never
compared against the oracle they replace -- the state
`com-abb-robotics`' own record calls `:not-claimed`.

`scripts/gen-parity-harness.cljs` closes that. This iteration ran it on three
more repositories and verified each against its own compiled artifact rather
than landing harnesses unverified, because a harness that has never been run is
the same 'built but never compared' state one level up.

## Reproduce, per repository

    kbb --backend sci scripts/gen-parity-harness.cljk <repo-dir> <src-dir-name>
    amu check   src/<ns>/whole_component.kotoba --jvm-free --policy policy.edn
    amu compile src/<ns>/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos \\
                --policy policy.edn --output a.kexe
    amu extract-native a.kexe --symbol <oracle> --output s.bin   # prints :offset
    KEXE_FUEL=100000000 kexe-loader s.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 7,23 <selector>
    kbb --backend sci --classpath src:<kotoba-lang/text>/src q9-oracle.cljs

## Result

`com-abb-800xa`, `com-accelbyte` and `com-ach-network`, none previously touched
by this work, each: `amu check :ok true`, compiled to aarch64-macos, executed,
and compared selector by selector.

    route count   30 on both sides
    as-bool       9 of 9
    page-limit    8 of 8
    as-int        5 of 6, the \"12abc\" row differing

Identical in all three. Coverage moves from five to eight of twenty.

## The differing row is the reason the other numbers can be read

`as-int` of `\"12abc\"` is 0 from each artifact and 12 from each oracle under nbb.
That is the cohort-wide reader-conditional divergence: the oracle's `:cljs`
branch is `js/parseInt` and its `:clj` branch is `Long/parseLong`, which throws
and yields 0, and every component matches `:clj`.

These are the sixth, seventh and eighth independent reproductions. More
usefully, in each run it is the only row that differs -- so a run in which
everything matched would not be a better result, it would be a reason to
suspect the harness never reached the artifact. The generated harness says so in
its own docstring rather than leaving the next reader to work it out.

## What the generator still refuses to emit

Only the route count and `as-int` / `as-bool` / `page-limit` over the shared
fixture table -- the layers derivable without a judgement call. The store,
filter, pagination, handler and expand layers need fixtures shaped to each
repository's entities, and the four hand-written harnesses disagree about them:
twenty sections in `com-aadhaar`, eleven in `com-abb-robotics`. An arm that runs
without asserting anything about its repository is worse than an absent one.

So eight of twenty is coverage of four layers, not of the components. That
distinction is worth keeping in view when this number is quoted.

## An operational note

Three worktree creations failed at the start of this iteration with `missing but
already registered worktree`. Earlier iterations had removed worktree
DIRECTORIES with `rm -rf` without `git worktree remove`, leaving git's
registrations behind. `git worktree prune` clears them. Removing a worktree by
deleting its directory looks like it worked and fails the next time the path is
reused.")))
